changes to the travel list have caused  huge frustration this summer but how  exactly are the decisions made well it's  assessed by a body called the joint  biosecurity center or the jbc it looks  at over 250 countries and territories  everywhere is assumed to be amber unless  there's evidence to suggest otherwise  the first stage is this variant  assessment it includes keeping tabs on  the transmissibility of strains and  whether they're vaccine resistant  secondly each country is triaged  assessed on set criteria and while that  does include a look at case numbers  they're also looking at things like  testing rates and whether a country has  the capacity to identify variants using  genomic sequencing and finally those  countries which emerge as contenders for  switching are given a more comprehensive  risk assessment then ministers make the  final decision  so there's a lot more going on than just  a look at straight case numbers and this  is partly why this shows the proportion  of positive cases to tests taken it  shows the situation in mexico and  pakistan they were both on the red list  and in both of those countries the pure  case numbers are actually lower than  they are here but that's because they're  testing proportionally fewer people  india and the uae it's worth noting  those two lines below the uk they were  downgraded to amber and those  proportions will certainly have helped  now keeping new variants out is  absolutely key this shows how in the uk  and in germany the delta variant the  light blue bit is totally dominant while  in france and spain the beta variant  that's the one which may have some  vaccine resistance has solid footholds  partly explains why germany has been  upgraded and france and spain haven't  been  in fact when it comes to variants what  we don't know is every bit as important  as what we do know the uk has some of  the best genomic sequencing in the world  as you can see we're testing for  variants far more frequently than our  european neighbors and finally i want to  leave you with this this shows just how  much we don't know there's been some  contention over why india is now amber  while pakistan remains red well pakistan  only tested genomes in 19 cases in the  most recent two weeks we have data for  19 cases in the whole country if a new  variant was taking hold authorities  would know little about it it shows how  these decisions are ultimately a complex  balance of risks
 

